{"subject":"Physiology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Physiology::12.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537250","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stem":"Which of the following can be used to calculate cardiac output? A. ECHO B. Ventilation – perfusion scan C. Thermodilution D. Fick’s principle","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A, B, C, D"},{"label":"B","text":"B, A, D"},{"label":"C","text":"B, C, D"},{"label":"D","text":"A, C, D"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"A, C, D","solutionHtml":"/span&amp;gt; Output\n<p>Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood ejected by the heart into the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ce68eab48a1692777181c719b4344e\">aorta</span> per minute. Several methods are used to measure CO, each with its own principle and considerations.</p>\n<h4><strong>Key Measurement Methods</strong></h4>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Method</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Principle</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Measurement Basis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Key Considerations</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Additional Details</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Fick Principle</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Oxygen <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cc20d2d7111692777201fb0caeb3f2\">uptake</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3c19af349e16927771825f314a4a84\">arteriovenous</span> difference</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Oxygen content in blood</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Accurate in steady states</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Cardiac output = <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"72d435bfb11692777194f88fdb0bf3\">Oxygen Consumption</span> / (Arterial Oxygen Content - <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">Venous</span> Oxygen Content)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Indicator <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"409e887c94169277718652d5db3c32\">Dilution</span> Method</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Indicator concentration dilution</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Injected <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e8a3764ab516927771863f3212fc41\">dye</span> or tracer</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Requires precise indicator handling</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Measures <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"409e887c94169277718652d5db3c32\">dilution</span> of an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a367ebd0c169277719091155a5b0f\">injected</span> dye/tracer over time to calculate <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> output.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Echocardiogram</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Ultrasound <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"33ba8d60fb16927772029d1067e602\">visualization</span> and flow velocity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Stroke volume estimation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Non-invasive, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9ddd195e9a1692777197bb3f135c2e\">real-time</span> data</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Estimates <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b7eed7d7e816927771995e0a067401\">stroke volume</span> from blood velocity, multiplied by heart rate for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"44758fcec31692777184e24fa84582\">CO</span> calculation.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Thermodilution</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Temperature change due to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a367ebd0c169277719091155a5b0f\">injected</span> fluid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Pulmonary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e5d34563e91692777183da2015189e\">catheter</span> insertion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Gold-standard, ideal for high <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> output states</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Measures temperature change in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"10e2dee19e1692777196673db90c23\">pulmonary</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> after injecting cold fluid.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Velocity Encoded MRI</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Magnetic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"37b9856df8169277719716b550a15f\">resonance</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fe3220767716927771892419444041\">imaging</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12c4510e191692777201c23ec126a3\">velocity</span> encoding</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Blood flow velocity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Non-invasive, requires advanced equipment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Uses <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7ce7bcb2f316927771938e0bea7f4d\">MRI</span> to measure <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"82cc227a1c169277718387905b50c6\">blood flow</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12c4510e191692777201c23ec126a3\">velocity</span> for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> output estimation.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Impedance Cardiography</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Electrical <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5506888d131692777189948dbb6f26\">impedance</span> changes</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Thoracic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5506888d131692777189948dbb6f26\">impedance</span> fluctuations</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Sensitive to external factors</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Measures electrical <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5506888d131692777189948dbb6f26\">impedance</span> across the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"30e8f90f0e16927772006cb55500e7\">thorax</span> to calculate <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> output.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, Edition 26, page- 532, 533</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537252","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stem":"What is the formula used to calculate Stroke Volume (SV) in the context of cardiac physiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SV = Heart Rate (HR) × Ejection Fraction (EF)"},{"label":"B","text":"SV = End-Diastolic Volume (EDV) - End-Systolic Volume (ESV)"},{"label":"C","text":"SV = Cardiac Output (CO) / Heart Rate (HR)"},{"label":"D","text":"SV = Preload × Afterload"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"SV = End-Diastolic Volume (EDV) - End-Systolic Volume (ESV)","solutionHtml":"<ul>\n<li>Stroke Volume (SV) refers to the amount of blood ejected by the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"707de303e516927772011cb80f4028\">ventricle</span> of the heart in a single contraction.</li>\n<li>The formula to calculate Stroke Volume is based on the difference between the End Diastolic Volume (EDV) and the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cba4457f2c16927771877052c293f6\">End-Systolic Volume</span> (ESV) and is around 70 ml.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Stroke Volume (SV)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Represents the blood ejected from the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"707de303e516927772011cb80f4028\">ventricle</span> per heartbeat.</li>\n<li>Determined by the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6f4a4d02ec169277718995b05c1d26\">horizontal</span> distance between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"437bfd6ffb1692777187b5a7a706d5\">end-diastolic volume</span> (EDV) and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cba4457f2c16927771877052c293f6\">end-systolic volume</span> (ESV).</li>\n<li>Formula: SV = EDV - ESV.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"phys1\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"11133\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"385\" src=\"images/exp_118_110_1.jpg\" width=\"400\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<br/><img src=\"images/exp_118_110_1.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, Edition 26, Pg 528, 529.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537254","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":3,"stem":"Which of the following jugular venous pressure (JVP) waves corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A wave"},{"label":"B","text":"C wave"},{"label":"C","text":"X descent"},{"label":"D","text":"Y descent"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"C wave","solutionHtml":"<ul>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"af7ff6c5fd1692777190f88c20b21b\">jugular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">venous</span> pressure (JVP) reflects the pressure within the right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a8d33da8541692777182ec6983d9a2\">atrium</span> and corresponds to various phases of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> cycle.</li>\n<li>The <strong>C wave</strong> of the JVP represents the <strong>isovolumetric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> phase</strong> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> cycle. It occurs during early <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> systole, as the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"416dd1004a169277720177d9a6adc5\">valve</span> bulges into the right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a8d33da8541692777182ec6983d9a2\">atrium</span> due to rising pressure in the right ventricle. This <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a6cf2c3dc51692777200ef7fb22f69\">transient</span> bulging causes a slight increase in the JVP.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Jugular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">Venous</span> Pressure (JVP) Waveform</strong></h3>\n<p> </p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:900px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>a wave </strong><strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Caused by atrial systole.</li>\n<li>Atrial <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> results in a temporary halt in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">venous</span> inflow.</li>\n<li>Some blood flows back into the veins, causing a rise in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">venous</span> pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>c wave</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> contraction.</li>\n<li>Pressure is transmitted through the closed and bulging <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> valve.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>x <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e9449b4d7a16927771867eb16e2878\">descent</span> </strong><strong>(Option C ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ventricular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13941ea4ec1692777199fc8cfd1a7a\">systole</span> pulls the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"416dd1004a169277720177d9a6adc5\">valve</span> down</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>v wave</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Corresponds to the rise in atrial pressure as blood fills the atria.</li>\n<li>Occurs during <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"11d448b6c4169277718635a922383c\">diastole</span> while the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"416dd1004a169277720177d9a6adc5\">valve</span> is still closed.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>y <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e9449b4d7a16927771867eb16e2878\">descent</span> </strong><strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Opening of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"416dd1004a169277720177d9a6adc5\">valve</span> causing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"397f16a7a71692777187ffffefa7e6\">emptying</span> of right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a8d33da8541692777182ec6983d9a2\">atrium</span> into ventricle</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"phys5\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"11139\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"190\" src=\"images/exp_118_114_1.jpg\" width=\"400\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n<br/><img src=\"images/exp_118_114_1.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, Edition 26, Pg 531</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537256","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":4,"stem":"The stroke volume depends on end-diastolic volume. This is best represented as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Length–weight relationship"},{"label":"B","text":"Length–velocity relationship"},{"label":"C","text":"Length-tension relationship"},{"label":"D","text":"Length-viscosity relationship"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Length-tension relationship","solutionHtml":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Stroke volume</strong> refers to the amount of blood ejected by the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"707de303e516927772011cb80f4028\">ventricle</span> during <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13941ea4ec1692777199fc8cfd1a7a\">systole</span> and is influenced by the <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong>, which determines the degree of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> stretch before contraction.</li>\n<li>This is explained by the <strong>Frank-Starling law of the heart</strong>, which states that the force of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> increases with greater <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52edf0af4d1692777202dd1a490884\">filling</span> (EDV) due to increased <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4621bd564d16927771984726dbd76c\">sarcomere</span> stretch.</li>\n<li>The <strong>length–tension relationship</strong> describes how the initial length of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> muscle fibers (determined by EDV) affects the tension they can generate during contraction, which directly impacts stroke volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>The Frank-Starling law:</strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>describes the relationship between the volume of blood <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52edf0af4d1692777202dd1a490884\">filling</span> the heart (end-diastolic volume) and the force of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> during the next <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"bf4c28aebb1692777188074f2facbb\">heartbeat</span> (stroke volume)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preload: Increased <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"96e7e3fecc16927772018cb9f8c8cd\">venous</span> return leads to increased end-diastolic volume.</li>\n<li>Myocardial Stretch: Greater <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"437bfd6ffb1692777187b5a7a706d5\">end-diastolic volume</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad337177ee1692777199de941a0cda\">stretches</span> the myocardial fibers.</li>\n<li>Force of Contraction: The stretched fibers generate a more forceful <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> due to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"208cf74d74169277719486afd05eb3\">optimal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ea564f2f5e1692777194df1391fd69\">overlap</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8e023fb81e16927771802d21820404\">actin</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"99c8e09d6f1692777193d7716ab6eb\">myosin</span> filaments (length-tension relationship).</li>\n</ul>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Guyton Textbook of Physiology 14th Edition, Page 123, 124, 245</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537258","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":5,"stem":"Arrange the following in the sequence in which they occur, after the 'p wave' in ECG: A- 'a' wave B- 1st heart sound C- Rapid filling of ventricles D- 't' wave","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABCD"},{"label":"B","text":"ABDC"},{"label":"C","text":"CABD"},{"label":"D","text":"ACBD"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"ABDC","solutionHtml":"The correct sequence after the P wave is as follows:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A. 'a' wave:</strong> The 'a' wave represents atrial contraction. It occurs during <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"67e25adce51692777182cac06e3c61\">atrial systole</span> when the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"422ada68fd16927771820019d5de1e\">atria</span> contract to push blood into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>B. 1st heart sound: </strong>The 1st heart sound, also known as S1, is the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2b4569285f1692777184282b1ca3f1\">closure</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d77b211e3a1692777182121ece27fc\">atrioventricular</span> (AV) valves (mitral and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ab13cdf64169277720085e2ebef1c\">tricuspid</span> valves) at the beginning of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> systole. It marks the onset of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>D. 't' wave:</strong> The 't' wave is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"40451a27bd1692777197d6c276d0bc\">repolarization</span> phase of the ventricles. It represents the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5bc49d94df16927771975eae86de9f\">relaxation</span> and resetting of the ventricles after contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>C. Rapid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52edf0af4d1692777202dd1a490884\">filling</span> of ventricles:</strong> After the 't' wave, the ventricles enter the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"11d448b6c4169277718635a922383c\">diastole</span> phase, during which they passively fill with blood from the atria. This is known as rapid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8cac43cc16927772015049bb5ab8\">ventricular</span> filling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct sequence is ABDC: 'a' wave, 1<sup>st</sup> heart sound, rapid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52edf0af4d1692777202dd1a490884\">filling</span> of ventricles, and 't' wave.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, 26th Edition- Page 519, 530, 531</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537260","topic":"PrepLadder · 12.Previous Year Questions","number":6,"stem":"Which of the following events occur immediately when a person changes his/her posture from supine to standing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in firing from baroreceptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Vagal output to cardiac fibers increase"},{"label":"C","text":"Mesenteric Vasoconstriction"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased cardiac contractility"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Decrease in firing from baroreceptor","solutionHtml":"ong&amp;gt;Baroreceptor Reflex\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Aspect</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Details</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Baroreceptors in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8593e48e0416927771834eaedb0bfd\">carotid</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b0046463171692777198e0beea83e7\">sinus</span> and aortic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9a89dd67841692777182ee412af263\">arch</span> detect BP changes</li>\n<li>Increased BP <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad337177ee1692777199de941a0cda\">stretches</span> receptors, sending signals to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"53abb386551692777192c4d1f1f5ed\">medulla</span> via <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13b0890d441692777188afdcb41e75\">glossopharyngeal</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c8da04ed171692777201da65246acd\">vagus</span> nerves</li>\n<li>Medulla inhibits <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"52abfe100916927772011ad20f3fda\">vasoconstrictor</span> center and stimulates <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fb29f3cd516927772017bd29c2581\">vagal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8f19a37b5916927771941ba9f5ef49\">parasympathetic</span> center <strong>(Option B ruled out)</strong></li>\n<li>It is a rapid negative <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4502f66ca416927772038bba0b4198\">feedback loop</span> in which an elevated <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"263ee5444e1692777183a7e8e704da\">blood pressure</span> causes the heart rate to decrease. Decreased <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"263ee5444e1692777183a7e8e704da\">blood pressure</span> decreases <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5f69a877f0169277718282c4697572\">baroreflex</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ba6b77f29316927771807350f86ce2\">activation</span> and causes heart rate to increase and to restore <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"263ee5444e1692777183a7e8e704da\">blood pressure</span> levels. <strong>(Option A)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Actions</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Vasodilation of blood vessels</li>\n<li>Decreased heart rate and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> strength <strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></li>\n<li>Reduced BP due to lower <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"255674f3271692777195c172d86daf\">peripheral</span> resistance and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e1fb96fd2416927771830dfed40e14\">cardiac</span> output</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Importance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Maintains BP stability during activities like standing</li>\n<li>Fastest acting</li>\n<li>Tends to reset within 1-2 days for long-term regulation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Baroreceptor &lt;span class=\" custommeta\"=\"\" data-dictid=\"20f478f55f1692777197e351415f8f\"/>Reflex - Supine to standing\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"12135\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"May2025\" height=\"320\" src=\"images/QLAOdtkOSmvd2OpYr7xO1746516919.png\"\" width=\"521\" /&amp;gt;</p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong’s Review of Medical Physiology, 26th Edition, Page 575, 576, 577,578, 579, 582, 583, 585, 586<br/>Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology, 14th Edition, Page 221, 222, 229, 236, 243, 244.</div>","tags":""}]}